VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-018
Reason for Visit: Severe, crampy calf pain when walking short distances, causing significant distress and immobility.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting new, severe calf pain on minimal exertion, raising the suspicion of acute or rapidly progressing peripheral arterial disease. While hyperlipidemia is often asymptomatic, its consequence (critical limb ischemia) requires urgent evaluation. Assessment could not be deferred until the next in-hours period to clinically rule out acute limb ischemia or deep vein thrombosis and to provide immediate pain management and lifestyle counseling.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Every time I walk to the letterbox, my calves cramp up so painfully I have to stop and rest for five minutes before I can move."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a progressive history of intermittent claudication indicating peripheral arterial disease over the last 6 months, which has acutely worsened over the past week. Pain occurs predictably after walking 50 meters and resolves completely with 5-10 minutes of rest. The patient is otherwise completely asymptomatic regarding cardiac or neurological symptoms. Denies rest pain, ulceration, or acute sudden loss of sensation in the legs. KEY SYMPTOMS: Intermittent claudication indicating peripheral arterial disease. Often completely asymptomatic and diagnosed incidentally (prior to this vascular complication). Xanthelasmas presenting as yellowish cholesterol deposits on the eyelids.
Functional Status: Mobility heavily restricted by claudication pain; fearful of leaving the house.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, independent living threatened by deteriorating mobility.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99 percent | Wt: 90 Kg
General: Overweight, comfortable at rest, no signs of acute distress.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. Carotid bruits noted on auscultation bilaterally. Peripheral Examination: Femoral pulses present but weak. Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are absent bilaterally. No acute pallor, paralysis, or coldness of the limbs (ruling out acute embolic occlusion). Skin/HEENT: Yellowish cholesterol deposits (Xanthelasmas) noted on the eyelids. A greyish-white ring (Corneal arcus) is visible. Palpable nodular swellings in the Achilles tendons (Tendon xanthomas). DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Tendon xanthomas, Xanthelasmas, Corneal arcus, Elevated serum LDL (anticipated on fasting bloods).
Environmental Observations: Sedentary environment, heavily processed food packaging visible in the kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: Not currently on any regular medications. Has avoided doctors for years.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Hyperlipidaemia (Complicated by symptomatic Peripheral Arterial Disease) - Status: Chronic, worsening
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is strictly exertional (claudication) rather than constant, and there is an absence of unilateral swelling, erythema, or a palpable tender cord.

Spinal Stenosis (Neurogenic Claudication) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is relieved simply by resting standing up, rather than requiring sitting/flexing of the spine, and is accompanied by profoundly absent distal pulses and bruits.

Acute Arterial Occlusion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of the "6 Ps" (pain at rest, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis, poikilothermia); the limb is viable and well-perfused at rest.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing severe, flow-limiting intermittent claudication as a direct complication of undiagnosed, severe systemic atherosclerosis, driven by underlying severe hyperlipidemia (evidenced by the striking physical stigmata of xanthelasma, arcus, and xanthomas). Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively differentiate this worsening claudication from an acute limb-threatening embolic occlusion or a deep vein thrombosis. Establishing that the limb is currently viable at rest allowed for safe outpatient management, precluding an unnecessary emergency department visit, but ensuring definitive vascular follow-up is initiated immediately.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated Aspirin 100mg daily. Ordered a comprehensive fasting lipid profile, HbA1c, and a resting 12-lead ECG for the following morning. Generated an urgent referral for Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI) testing and Vascular Surgery review.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to begin a structured walking program: walk exactly to the point of moderate calf pain, stop and rest until it completely resolves, and then continue. By intentionally pushing to the pain threshold and resting, the patient stimulates the growth of new collateral blood vessels. The reward is a measurable, progressive increase in the distance they can walk before the pain starts.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Routine General Practice review in 3 days to review lipid results and commence high-intensity statin therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the leg pain becomes constant while resting in a chair or bed, or if the foot suddenly turns pale, cold, or numb, the patient must attend the nearest Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of the specific lipid disorder requires fasting blood tests; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The claudication is a symptom of widespread plaque buildup. Without aggressive medical therapy (statins, antiplatelets) and lifestyle changes, the risk of heart attack or stroke is exceedingly high.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Pace your walking. Stop and rest when it hurts. Go to the hospital immediately if your foot turns cold, pale, or hurts continuously while resting.

